interviewing everyone from Led  Zeppelin's Jimmy Page to NFL superstar  Tom Brady Chuck Klosterman span of work  is varied to say the least  Chuck is a best-selling author of eight  non-fiction books and two novels he's  also written for publications like the  New York Times and GQ his latest work  Chuck Klosterman ten a highly specific  defiantly incomplete history of the  early 21st century is a collection of  columns and articles published over the  past decade Chuck Klosterman good  morning welcome great to be here thanks  for having me on you cover a lot of  ground in here one of the the early  essays that you have in this book is  about zombies that you were commissioned  to write by the New York Times I think  and you say in a lot of modern life is  exactly exactly like slaughtering  zombies yeah well the conceit about that  was you know everybody now seems to have  an understanding of if there is a zombie  apocalypse how to deal with it so it's  widely understood you just like you take  a shotgun or some kind of axe and you  just keep kind of knocking them down as  they come at you over and over again and  as I was writing this I was like that's  sort of like dealing with email it's  reference well example because it you'll  never finish right you know they'll  never be a point where you're there'll  be no more emails to look at or no more  things to sort of check on so you live  your life sort of in a technology-based  world like you're destroying zombies  you're just in a corner trying to  survive by in a Nile aiding them one by  one it feels like the zombies actually  are more deadly than those emails might  be maybe I'm not certain in that  actually in the intro to that you also  wrote about getting the piece and sort  of it had you said you were a big fan of  the work they would have given it to you  had you said as you did that you weren't  they gave it to you and I thought that  in all these intros to your stories you  really did a great job of sort of  exploring something about each one did  you learn a lot about your stories and  looking back to put this together well I  suppose a little bit I mean you can only  learn so much about work you did  yourself right like you can't you can't  read something you wrote and then be  surprised by it um but what I just try  to do is don't I IRA publish these  pieces because they have run elsewhere  their people have seen them before  but you know everything is it's the  commedia is such a constructed reality  that I always think  interesting if you can tell people just  a little bit of how it actually is like  how these things actually come into  being and what the experience of meeting  these people is actually like because  the stories sometimes don't reflect that  yeah they sort of get changed by the way  by like the manufacturing process of  magazines or the internet or whatever  there's a number of great music stories  in the book and I was surprised to see  that you were a big fan of KISS you say  social the social footprint they leave  is deeper than their catalog yeah I mean  kiss is my favorite band to think about  okay well The Beatles are my favorite  band to listen to but this is my  favorite band to think about I think  that they are the most interesting band  in a way it's almost like you can  understand the entire concept and sort  of the unreality of rock music through  the trajectory of their career and you  know I was into kiss when I was young  and I never really stopped I think  what's really interesting about being  into kisses you can have almost an  adversarial relationship with the band  but it's not like Bruce Springsteen  right where you think to yourself oh boy  if you know when he's not on tour he's  probably working at a car wash in New  Jersey you know this lion is Asian with  kiss you can make fun of them while you  like them you also had a lot of sports  stories in here Tom Brady and Kobe  Bryant couldn't have been to more  different interviews for you either I  mean in practice absolutely I mean Kobe  Bryant I went assuming it was going to  be a real difficult thing and he wanted  to talk about his life in a way that was  so lucid and upfront I mean I've never  had an interview with the sports athlete  yeah I'm reading it you hear more than  you normally and it just immediately  happened it's not like I spent a month  with him like is like 80 minutes and he  told me more in like 80 minutes than  most people I have talked to you for two  years but Tom Brady was the complete  opposite the whole reason I was doing  that interview I thought was to ask him  these questions about deflategate and  that the whole thing was set up so that  he would sort of address these issues  that he'd never lead rest before but  that wasn't the case at all I mean maybe  somebody said he was gonna do that but  he didn't know that how do you confront  that situation when you're when you I  mean we've all sort  Bennett I'm curious how you confront  that when you when somebody puts up a  wall like that well the thing is is when  I realize that this just wasn't going to  happen and that the central thing that  we were supposed to be discussing was  not going to be part of the conversation  at all for a while I thought well maybe  this story will just be eliminated maybe  it'll just evaporate but it was for  gqueues Man of the Year issue and Tom  Brady was the man of the year but so  that's not good this story was going to  run even if he had never called me at  all so then I said well how about this  I'll write an essay about Tom Brady and  then in the middle I will include the  Q&amp;A section of when I ask him these  questions no he refuses to answer but I  mean certainly there is something you  learn from someone when they say nothing  I mean the way that they react to  questions even if they're not giving you  information does maybe give you more  insight into what their personality is  really like as opposed to if they had a  constructed answer that they gave you  that is really no different than you  know public relations yeah Chuck we  appreciate the time thank you some great  stuff great work even you've seen it  before thank you

